    },
    {
      "claim": "Doravirine, an N-methyl heteroaromatic chlorophenyl drug, has absolute F of 64% from a 100 mg tablet and modeled F of 0.82-0.895, with no significant food effect, supporting high F under the fed state.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C06","C07","C11","C41"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Chlorophenyl triazolobenzodiazepine analogs show human oral F of 44% to 100%, all above the 20% threshold.",
      "card_ids": ["C05","C16","C17","C20","C23","C25","C28","C10","C12","C13","C18","C33","C40","C43"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Trazodone, a chlorophenyl heteroaromatic lactam with a basic amine, has absolute oral F of 65% to 90% in humans and high F in dogs.",
      "card_ids": ["C02","C21","C24","C27","C35"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Additional multi-species exposure records in the same contexts are supportive: baclofen dog F 0.69, a doravirine analog F 52% in dogs, CPI-0610 good across species, MK-8628 orally bioavailable, and fasudil good oral bioavailability.",
      "card_ids": ["C34","C39","C36","C45","C37"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The only low F values are rat-specific (alprazolam 4.5%, fasudil 9.46%, baclofen metabolites very low) and are not transferable to the human endpoint; alprazolam and fasudil are contradicted by human absolute F values of 92% and 69% in the same contexts.",
      "card_ids": ["C30","C42","C32","C05","C20","C28","C08"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral AUC/Cmax records for midazolam, etoricoxib, and indomethacin report only exposure values without IV comparators or F estimates and are structurally distant from the query; they do not provide threshold-relevant evidence.",
      "card_ids": ["C46","C47","C48","C49","C50","C51","C52","C53","C54","C55","C56","C57"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "oral_auc_cmax_exposure",
      "applicability": "low",
      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C46","C47","C48","C49","C50","C51","C52","C53","C54","C55","C56","C57"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct query-specific absolute oral bioavailability measurement exists.",
    "No query-specific fed-state pharmacokinetic data; food effect is inferred from doravirine and baclofen analogs.",
    "L3 oral AUC/Cmax records lack IV comparators and are for structurally distant molecules, so they do not provide F-threshold evidence.",
    "Structural transferability is qualitative; no numeric similarity score is supplied.",
    "Physicochemical liabilities such as high logP and poor solubility remain but are not affirmative evidence for F < 20%."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Prediction remains high. L1/L2 analog evidence from tasquinimod, doravirine, triazolobenzodiazepines, and trazodone supports F >= 20%; the only low values are rat-specific and contradicted by human absolute F in the same contexts. L3 oral AUC/Cmax records for midazolam, etoricoxib, and indomethacin are not threshold-relevant or transferable and do not change the decision."
}

